  <published>2019-03-02T00:00:00Z</published>
  
  <category term="Blog"></category>
  
  <category term="Hardware"></category>
  
  <category term="RISC OS"></category>
  
  <category term="Risc PC"></category>
  
  <link href="http://www.davespace.co.uk/blog/20190225-riscpc-feet.html" rel="alternate"></link>
  <content type="html"><![CDATA[
    
    
    
<p>If you have a faithful Acorn Risc PC kicking around you might have found that the rubber feet on the bottom of the case have started to crumble, like mine:</p>
<img style="border-radius: 1em" src="/blog/images/bumpons/broken.jpg" alt="A close-up picture of the underside of an inverted Risc PC with a crumbling rubber foot.">
<p>In some cases the rubber feet can even liquefy and leave you with a wonky computer sitting in a pool of grey goo.</p>
<p>You&rsquo;ll be pleased to know that you can lever off the old ones and replace them with a new set of <em>Bumpons</em> (that&rsquo;s what they&rsquo;re called) from 3M.</p>

    
    
  ]]></content>
</entry>



<entry>
